---
import SiteLayout from "../../../../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../../../../../../components/Eyebrow.astro";
---
<SiteLayout title="Module 4: Physical Layer — fred.fish" description="Source-based physical-layer lessons on signals, copper, fiber, wireless, and media selection." active="cisco">
  <section class="source-lesson">
    <div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
    <header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 04" label="MODULE 4 / PHYSICAL LAYER" /><h1>Move the bits.<br /><span>Understand the medium.</span></h1><p>The physical layer turns frames into signals that can travel over a medium. The medium and signaling method determine practical distance, speed, and environmental limits.</p></header>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>What the physical layer does</h2><p>The physical layer defines how raw bits are represented and transmitted. It specifies the physical components, connectors, pinouts, cable types, radio frequencies, optical properties, and signaling or encoding methods. It does not interpret IP addresses or make forwarding decisions. Its job is to move the bit stream across the link so the receiving device can recover it.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0363 · IMG-0371</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Measure network performance</h2><p>Bandwidth is the theoretical capacity of a link, commonly expressed in bits per second. Throughput is the actual rate achieved over time. Goodput measures useful application data delivered, excluding protocol overhead and retransmissions. Latency is the time required for data to travel from source to destination. A high-bandwidth link can still perform poorly when congestion, errors, or latency are high.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE TOPIC</span><code>Physical-layer performance / bandwidth</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Copper cabling</h2><p>Copper carries data as electrical signals. Common Ethernet media include unshielded twisted pair (UTP), shielded twisted pair (STP), and coaxial cable in some environments. Twisting helps reduce electromagnetic interference and crosstalk between wire pairs. Shielding can provide additional protection in electrically noisy environments, but requires appropriate grounding and installation. Copper is practical and inexpensive, but distance and interference limit its performance.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0354 · IMG-0368 · IMG-0375 · IMG-0355 · IMG-0369 · IMG-0373</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Fiber-optic cabling</h2><p>Fiber carries information as pulses of light through glass or plastic strands. It is resistant to electromagnetic interference and supports high bandwidth over long distances. Single-mode fiber uses a very small core and is suited to long-distance links. Multimode fiber has a larger core and is commonly used for shorter links such as within buildings or data centers. Fiber requires compatible optical transceivers and careful connector cleaning and handling.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-0351 · IMG-0358 · IMG-0372 · IMG-0378</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">05</div><div><h2>Wireless media</h2><p>Wireless networks transmit information through electromagnetic waves rather than a physical cable. Radio frequency (RF) is used by Wi-Fi and many other wireless technologies. Wireless links are affected by distance, obstacles, interference, channel overlap, antenna placement, and regulatory limits. A wireless design trades cabling convenience for a shared and more variable radio environment.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0354 · IMG-0368 · IMG-0375</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">06</div><div><h2>Select the medium for the environment</h2><p>Media selection should account for the distance the signal must travel and the environment where the medium will be installed. Also evaluate required bandwidth, interference, physical protection, installation complexity, compatible interfaces, and total cost. Use copper where its reach and electrical environment are suitable; choose fiber for longer distances, higher capacity, or electrical isolation; choose wireless where mobility or cabling constraints justify the shared radio medium.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGE</span><code>IMG-0353 · IMG-0359 · IMG-0360</code></div></div></section>
    <section class="media-comparison">
  <h2>Compare the three common media types</h2>
  <div class="media-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable media comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Medium</th><th>Signal</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Copper</strong></td><td>Electrical</td><td>Low cost, easy termination, common Ethernet interfaces</td><td>Distance limits, EMI, and crosstalk</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Fiber</strong></td><td>Light</td><td>Long reach, high capacity, EMI immunity</td><td>Optical transceivers, careful handling, higher installation cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Wireless</strong></td><td>Radio / microwave</td><td>Mobility and flexible deployment</td><td>Interference, shared airtime, coverage and security planning</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Choose by distance, required throughput, electrical environment, mobility, installation constraints, and total cost.</p>
</section>
<section class="lesson-lab"><div class="lesson-number">LAB</div><div><h2>Diagnose a physical-link problem</h2><p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A workstation negotiates a 100 Mb/s link on a switch port that should support 1 Gb/s. The cable run is within the Ethernet distance limit, but the link repeatedly drops and the port reports errors.</p><ol><li>Check the negotiated speed and duplex on both ends. A mismatch can cause poor performance even when the link appears up.</li><li>Inspect connectors, cable damage, patch panels, and termination. Replace the patch cable with a known-good cable.</li><li>Check interface counters for CRC/FCS errors and link transitions. Increasing errors suggest a physical or duplex issue.</li><li>Retest after changing one variable. Confirm the expected speed and that error counters stop increasing.</li></ol><p class="lab-note">Do not assume every slow link is a bandwidth-capacity problem. Physical errors, negotiation, and duplex settings can reduce usable throughput.</p></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Choose the right physical medium.</h2><p>Practice cable types, signal behavior, performance measurements, and media selection.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
